raphic findings of reduced left ventricular ejection fraction and the presence of a mass. The irregular heart rhythm suggests atrial fibrillation, likely due to the mass obstructing normal atrial function. The mid-diastolic murmur further supports the diagnosis of a myxoma, as it indicates obstruction at the mitral valve level. The weight loss, while concerning for malignancy, may also be attributed to the chronic effects of the cardiac mass on the patient's overall health. In terms of histological findings, a biopsy of the mass is expected to show characteristics consistent with a myxoma, including bland cells in a myxoid stroma, which aligns with the clinical suspicion of a benign tumor causing significant cardiac and pulmonary symptoms. \n"], "raw_output": "'''Option: B'''"}
