{"question": "A 53-year-old man presents to clinic with a six month history of shoulder pain that is especially bothersome at night. Over the weekend he \"strained his shoulder\" during a pick-up basketball game and reports an acute exacerbation of his pain symptoms. On exam, he complains of pain to palpation just below the acromion. You suspect he has torn his supraspinatus. If correct, which of these functional maneuvers would you expect to be deficient on physical exam?", "options": {"A": "Initiation of adduction", "B": "External rotation", "C": "Initiation of abduction", "D": "Completion of abduction", "E": "Internal rotation"}, "pred_answer": "C", "gold_answer": "C", "question_domains": ["Orthopedics", "Sports Medicine", "Rheumatology", "Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation", "Diagnostic Imaging"], "option_domains": ["Orthopedics", "Physical Therapy"], "question_analyses": {"Orthopedics": "The scenario presented describes a 53-year-old man with a history of shoulder pain, particularly at night, which has been exacerbated by a recent acute injury during a basketball game. The location of the pain, specifically below the acromion, along with the suspicion of a supraspinatus tear, suggests a potential rotator cuff injury. ### Interpretation of the Condition:\nThe supraspinatus is one of the four rotator cuff muscles, and it plays a crucial role in shoulder abduction and stabilization of the glenohumeral joint. A tear in the supraspinatus can lead to significant functional impairment and pain, particularly during activities that require overhead motion or lifting the arm away from the body. ### Functional Maneuvers Likely to be Deficient:\nIn the case of a supraspinatus tear, the following functional maneuvers would typically be deficient on physical examination:\n\n1. **Abduction of the Arm**: The patient would likely have difficulty abducting the arm, especially from 0 to 90 degrees, as the supraspinatus is primarily responsible for initiating this movement. 2. *